"CONSUMER CONTRACTS\n(finding that business provided sufficient notice of its terms of service when it placed a sign-up\nbox with the text \"By signing up, I agree to Airbnb's Terms of Service,\" in the middle of the page,\nin contrasting, appropriately sized font, \"unobscured by other visual elements\"); Meyer v. Uber\nTechs., 868 F.3d 66 (2d Cir. 2017) (concluding that business's app satisfied the test of providing\nreasonably conspicuous notice of the Terms of Service as a matter of California law); Emmanuel\nv. Handy Techs., Inc., 992 F.3d 1, 9 (1st Cir. 2021) (deciding that consumer had reasonable notice\nof the terms because \"the screen displaying the portion of the Agreement that was plainly visible\nbefore [consumer] selected 'Accept' made clear that additional text further specifying the terms of\nthe Agreement could be viewed by scrolling\"); Immediato v. Postmates, Inc., 2021 WL 828381,\nat *4 (D. Mass. Mar. 4, 2021) (finding the seller provided reasonable notice because consumers\nwere required to click on a link that was prominently displayed beneath a legend that stated,\n\"Before continuing, you'll need to agree to the following terms,\" and because, after opening the\nlink, consumers were required to click an \"Agree\" button to show assent to terms); Claussen v.\nOnline Diamonds Int'l Corp., 2021 WL 39598 (N.Y. Sup. Ct. Jan. 5, 2021) (finding that terms\nwere reasonably conspicuous because they were presented as hyperlinks in blue font against a\ncontrasting background in an otherwise uncluttered web page); Starke v. SquareTrade, Inc., 913\nF.3d 279, 289 (2d Cir. 2019) (finding that the terms and conditions sent via an email after consumer\nmanifested assent to the transaction did not provide reasonable notice because seller's\nconfirmation email failed to draw any attention to the Terms & Conditions hyperlink, which was\ninconspicuously placed in small font at the bottom of the email); Foster v. Walmart, Inc. 15 F.4th\n860, 864 (8th Cir. 2021) (reversing the lower court's finding of insufficient notice of hyperlinked\nbrowsewrap terms and remanding so that the court could \"determine whether a reasonably prudent\nuser would have known to inquire further\" by examining \"the exact location and prominence of\nthe terms-of-use hyperlink, how many clicks it would have taken for the user to discover the\narbitration provision, and whether the website changed during the relevant period\"); In re Juul\nLabs, Inc., 555 F. Supp. 3d 932 (N.D. Cal. 2021) (finding that presenting an uncluttered clickbox\nwith a clear notice immediately below it explaining that clicking on the box constituted assent to\nthe terms and conditions and privacy policy, both hyperlinked in contrasting colors and in different\nfont, provided sufficient notice of the terms). Several Illustrations in this Section are based on\nleading cases that examined the reasonableness of the notice provided to consumers. Illustration\n13 is based on Specht v. Netscape, 306 F.3d 17 (2d Cir. 2002). Illustration 16 is based on\nHoldbrook Pediatric Dental, LLC v. Pro Computer Service, LLC, 2015 WL 4476017, at *6 (D.N.J.\nJuly 21, 2015) (finding that the \"existence of the hyperlink in the document [from PCS], without\nany statement to draw attention to the link, is insufficient to demonstrate that Holdbrook had\n'reasonable notice' that the 'Terms and Conditions' were part of the contract\"). It embodies the\nprinciple that incorporation by reference of a separate document must include a clear reference to\na specific document that can be accessed without undue burden. See also State ex rel. U-Haul Co.\nof W. Va. v. Zakaib, 752 S.E.2d 586, 589 (W. Va. 2013) (holding that \"[t]o uphold the validity of\nterms in a document incorporated by reference, (1) the writing must make a clear reference to the\nother document so that the parties' assent to the reference is unmistakable; (2) the writing must\n53\n@§2\n"